 37	\title{The sparse variance of squarefree numbers in arithmetic progressions to polynomial moduli: an elementary asymptotic formula}
 38	\author{Write-up of an archived machine-generated proof run\\(provenance in Appendix~\ref{app:provenance})}
 39	\date{}
 40	
 41	\begin{document}
 42	\maketitle
 43	
 44	\begin{abstract}
 45	For a polynomial $f\in\Z[t]$ of degree $d\ge1$ with $f(t)\ge2$ and $f'(t)\ge1$ for $t\ge1$, consider
 46	\[
 47	V_f(x,y)=\sum_{1\le k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{l=1}^{f(k)}\bigl(Q(x;f(k),l)-g(f(k),l)\,x\bigr)^2,
 48	\]
 49	where $Q(x;q,l)$ counts squarefree $n\le x$ with $n\equiv l\pmod q$ and $g(q,l)$ is the corresponding density.
 50	We show by elementary means that
 51	$V_f(x,y)=C_f\,x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}\bigl(1+O(x^{-\eta})\bigr)$ uniformly for $x^{7/9+\delta}\le f(y)\le x^{1-\delta}$, with an explicit Euler product $C_f>0$ and $\eta=\min(\delta/12,1/(6d))$.
 52	We also give explicit error terms valid throughout $f(y)\le x$, and a version with an exact arithmetic main term.
 53	The proof rests on an exact identity for the variance with respect to a single modulus, a uniform count of pairs of squarefree numbers with a given difference, an Euler--Maclaurin analysis of sawtooth sums $\sum_s B(Z/s^2)$, and an average of a multiplicative function over polynomial values.
 54	We compare the result with exact numerical computations of $V_f$ for $f=k^2+1$, $k^3+2$ and $2k+1$ up to $x=2.56\cdot10^8$.
 55	\end{abstract}
