  1	\section{Numerical evidence}\label{sec:numerics}
  2	
  3	This section compares \cref{thm:main} with exact computations of $V_f(x,y)$. Everything here is evidence and plays no part in the proofs. All tables and plots are regenerated by \nolinkurl{manuscript/reproduce.py} (see \nolinkurl{manuscript/README.md}). That script re-runs the accepted computation from its source code and recompiles its C\texttt{++} part; it also computes the theoretical quantities.
  4	
  5	\subsection{What was computed}\label{sec:num-method}
  6	
  7	\emph{Grid.} The three polynomials are $f(k)=k^2+1$, $k^3+2$ and $2k+1$, with weights $f'(k)=2k$, $3k^2$ and $2$. All three satisfy~\eqref{eq:f-hyp}. The values of $x$ are $x=2.5\cdot10^5\cdot2^j$ for $0\le j\le10$, so the largest is $x=2.56\cdot10^8$. For each $x$ and each target ratio $r\in\{0.01,0.02,0.05,0.1,0.2,0.4,0.7,1\}$, $y$ is the largest integer with $f(y)\le rx$. At the two largest $x$ the ratio $r=0.005$ was added as a diagnostic. The quantity reported is the normalised variance
  8	\[
  9	N(x,r)=\frac{V_f(x,y)}{x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}},
 10	\]
 11	computed with the actual ratio $f(y)/x$. By \cref{thm:main}(iii), $N(x,r)\to C_f$ when $x\to\infty$ and $f(y)/x\to0$ suitably.
 12	
 13	\emph{Method.} The computation (archived numerical snapshot, files \texttt{compute.py} and \texttt{counts.cpp}) is exact in all integer quantities.
 14	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
 15	\item The squarefree indicator $\mu^2(n)$, $n\le x$, is sieved. All autocorrelations
 16	\[
 17	C(h)=\sum_{1\le n\le x-h}\mu^2(n)\mu^2(n+h),\qquad0\le h\le x,
 18	\]
 19	are obtained from one number-theoretic transform modulo the prime $p_0=3221225473$, with primitive root~$5$. Every $C(h)$ is at most $x<p_0$, so no information is lost.
 20	\item As in the proof of \cref{prop:identity}, $\sum_lQ(x;q,l)^2=Q(x)+2\sum_{m\ge1}C(mq)$ for every modulus $q=f(k)$.
 21	\item The densities are $g(q,l)=\frac6{\pi^2}\tilde g(q,l)$ with an explicit rational $\tilde g(q,l)$ equivalent to \cref{prop:densities}(i),(iii). The coefficients $\sum_lQ(x;q,l)\tilde g(q,l)$ and $\sum_l\tilde g(q,l)^2$ are computed as exact rationals, using the counts of squarefree multiples of $d$ as in the proof of \cref{prop:trivial}.
 22	\item Finally $V(x;q)=\sum_lQ^2-2x\sum_lgQ+x^2\sum_lg^2$, as in~\eqref{eq:id1}, is accumulated with the weights $f'(k)$ in $70$-digit decimal arithmetic.
 23	\end{enumerate}
 24	The snapshot validates the implementation in several ways. It checks the transform against direct lag sums at every $x$. It compares $\tilde g$ with brute-force local densities for all $q\le50$ and with empirical frequencies at $x=10^6$. At $x=257$ it compares every generated modulus with direct residue counts. During the original run, the operator and an integrator also recomputed some rows independently by direct counting (Appendix~\ref{app:provenance}).
 25	
 26	\emph{Re-run.} For this write-up the snapshot code was run again, unchanged, after its SHA-256 hashes had been checked against the acceptance record. The C\texttt{++} part was compiled locally with \texttt{clang++ -O3}. All $270$ rows ($90$ per polynomial, including the diagnostic rows) reproduce the archived integer counts exactly. The values of $V_f$ agree to all stored digits (largest relative difference $\RerunMaxRelV$), and all fitted quantities are identical. The full grid needs about $15$ minutes and $5$\,GB of memory.
 27	
 28	\subsection{The constants \texorpdfstring{$C_f$}{C\_f}}\label{sec:num-constants}
 29	
 30	For the three polynomials the root counts are:
 31	\begin{itemize}
 32	\item $k^2+1$: $\rho(2)=1$, $\rho(4)=0$, and $\rho(p)=\rho(p^2)=1+\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)$ for odd $p$;
 33	\item $k^3+2$: $\rho(2)=\rho(3)=1$, $\rho(4)=\rho(9)=0$; for $p\ge5$, $\rho(p^2)=\rho(p)$, with $\rho(p)=1$ if $p\equiv2\pmod 3$, and, if $p\equiv1\pmod3$, $\rho(p)=3$ or $0$ according as $(-2)^{(p-1)/3}\equiv1\pmod p$ or not;
 34	\item $2k+1$: $\rho(2)=\rho(4)=0$, and $\rho(p)=\rho(p^2)=1$ for odd $p$.
 35	\end{itemize}
 36	For primes not dividing the discriminant all roots are simple, and each root modulo $p$ lifts to exactly one root modulo $p^2$, which gives $\rho(p^2)=\rho(p)$. The formulas were checked by brute force modulo $p$ and $p^2$ for all $p<\RhoBound$. None of the three polynomials has a content prime, so $P_0=\emptyset$, and~\eqref{eq:sigma-close} holds for every $p$ with $K=3+2d$. Hence, for $N\ge30$,
 37	\[
 38	\Bigl|\log\prod_{p>N}\sigma_p\Bigr|\le\frac{K}{1-K/N^2}\sum_{p>N}\frac1{p^2}\le\frac{8K}{1-K/N^2}\Bigl(\frac1{N^2}+\frac1{30N}\Bigr).
 39	\]
 40	Here we used that every prime $p>N\ge30$ lies in one of the $8$ reduced residue classes modulo $30$, and that each class contributes at most $N^{-2}+(30N)^{-1}$. \Cref{tab:constants} lists the partial values $\frac23I\prod_{p\le N}\sigma_p$ and the resulting interval for $C_f$ with $N=10^7$. The intervals account rigorously for the omitted primes; floating-point rounding, of relative size far below $10^{-12}$, is not included.
 41	
