 57	
 58	Second, $N(x,r)$ depends clearly on $r$. It rises from about $0.140$ at $r=0.005$ to between $0.167$ and $0.184$ at $r=1$. A fit of $\log V_f$ against $\log f(y)$ at fixed $x$ therefore gives an exponent $\beta\approx1.54$--$1.55$ instead of $\frac32$ (\cref{tab:fits}). This does not contradict \cref{thm:main}. Part (iii) concerns $f(y)/x\to0$, and at a fixed ratio $r$ the error term $x^{1/3+\eps}Y^{5/3}$ of part (i) is as large as the main term. The ratio dependence is captured by the exact-shape statement~(ii); see \cref{sec:num-shape}.
