172	\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:num-limits}
173	
174	\begin{itemize}
175	\item The computations stop at $x=2.56\cdot10^8$. Since $x^{-2/9}\approx0.0135$ at this $x$, the rows with $r<0.0135$ lie below $x^{7/9}$, outside the range of \cref{thm:main}(iii) even for $\delta\to0$. The rows with $r=1$ violate $Y\le x^{1-\delta}$ for every $\delta>0$, and $r\ge0.1$ requires $\delta\le\log10/\log x\approx0.12$. The implied constants in \cref{thm:main} are not explicit, so no finite computation can confirm or refute it. The data can only be checked for consistency.
176	\item The extrapolated constants depend on an empirical model, and their uncertainties are heuristic envelopes, not confidence intervals. The agreement in \cref{tab:fits} is therefore qualitative evidence for the value of $C_f$, and the theorem does not rest on it.
177	\item The values of $C_f$ in \cref{tab:constants} come with a rigorous bound for the omitted primes, but not with a certified bound for floating-point rounding.
178	\item The exact-shape comparison uses double-precision arithmetic, with $80$-digit evaluation only where cancellation occurs. Its agreement with the identity self-test is the main check of the implementation. For $2k+1$ it covers only $x\le10^6$.
179	\end{itemize}
