 23	\emph{Workflow.} The problem statement restricts $f$ to $\Z[t]$. The numerical candidates were computed before any plan existed, and one was accepted on computational grounds only, not because of its fitted constants. Eight proof plans were written, and the plan \texttt{simplest@claude-opus-5-5} was selected by the project owner. It splits the argument into nine propositions P1--P9. In this text, P1 is \cref{prop:densities}, P2 is \cref{prop:identity}, P3 is \cref{prop:pairs}, P4 is \cref{prop:trivial}, P5 is \cref{prop:sawtooth}, P6 is \cref{prop:MT}, P7 is \cref{prop:single}, P8 is \cref{prop:poly}, and P9 is \cref{thm:main} with its proof in \cref{sec:assembly}. All nine proofs passed both referees in the first round, with only cosmetic remarks. Both integrators found that the propositions compose to the stated theorem without loss and without interface mismatches. The project owner accepted the proof as correct and complete. A separate read-only operator audit found no substantive gap and listed local corrections, which are carried out in this write-up (see \texttt{changes.md}) together with the cosmetic points raised by the referees. Agreement between models is evidence, not certification; the arguments are reproduced here in full so that they can be checked directly.
