128	\subsection{The exact-shape statement}\label{sec:num-shape}
129	
130	\Cref{thm:main}(ii) replaces $C_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$ by $\Sigma_{\MT}=\sum_{k\le y}f'(k)\MT(x;f(k))$. We evaluated $\Sigma_{\MT}$ for the rows listed in \cref{tab:shape}, together with $\sum_{k\le y}F(k)h(f(k))$, the main term of \cref{prop:single}(ii) summed over the moduli. We report
131	\[
132	N_{\MT}=\frac{\Sigma_{\MT}}{x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}},\qquad N_h=\frac{1}{Y^{3/2}}\sum_{k\le y}F(k)h(f(k)).
133	\]
134	$\MT(x;q)$ is computed from the factorisation $t=t_0t_1t_2$ of the proof of \cref{prop:MT}. For $t_0\le\sqrt{Z_1}$ the terms are summed directly. For $t_0>\sqrt{Z_1}$ we use $B(u)=u-u^2$ ($u<1$) and the closed forms
135	\[
136	A_0\sum_{(t,q)=1}\eta(t)=\frac6{\pi^2}\prod_{p\mid q}\frac{p^2-2}{p^2-1},\qquad A_0\sum_{(t,q)=1}\frac{\eta(t)}{t^2}=\frac{36}{\pi^4}\prod_{p\mid q}\frac{p^2(p^2-2)}{(p^2-1)^2},
137	\]
138	which follow from~\eqref{eq:euler} as in \cref{lem:eta}. The first $64$ terms of the subtracted partial sums are evaluated in $80$-digit arithmetic; $A_0$ and $\prod_pa_p$ are evaluated to high precision through the prime zeta function. The infinite series~\eqref{eq:MT} is thus evaluated without truncation, up to floating-point rounding. As an independent check, \cref{prop:identity} was verified with this implementation of $\MT$ against brute-force values of every other term, for $x=3000$ and all $q\le40$ (largest relative discrepancy $\SelfTest$). For $2k+1$ the number of moduli is about $x/2$, so only $x\le10^6$ was treated.
