  1	\section{Provenance}\label{app:provenance}
  2	
  3	The mathematics in \cref{sec:prelim,sec:densities,sec:identity,sec:pairs,sec:sawtooth,sec:single,sec:poly,sec:assembly} is an edited presentation of an archived run of a multi-agent proof pipeline in which language models proposed, proved and refereed the results. This appendix records where the material comes from. A proposition-by-proposition map, with every editorial change and correction, is in \texttt{manuscript/changes.md}.
  4	
  5	\emph{Archive.}
  6	\begin{itemize}
  7	\item Source repository: \url{https://gitlab.com/mshepherd/squarefree-numbers}, archive label \texttt{zt-neutral}.
  8	\item Archive commit: \texttt{ed0aebaf461b602601856bf1b693fd76893d9aba}.
  9	\item Harness commit: \texttt{33aadc609359221ba5d81664438bce5f658f561c}.
 10	\item Revision recorded for all run stages: \texttt{8dfc53d01e56c9949c42061fdbe907a98a12a5d7}.
 11	\end{itemize}
 12	
 13	\emph{Models and roles (as recorded).} The model identifiers are those recorded in the archive. The command-line tool versions are not model-weight versions, and model versions beyond these identifiers are unknown.
 14	\begin{itemize}[before=\raggedright]
 15	\item Planning and probing: \texttt{claude-opus-5-5} and \texttt{codex:gpt-6.1-sol}; probing also \texttt{claude-sonnet-5-5}.
 16	\item Numerics: \texttt{claude-sonnet-5-5} and \texttt{codex:gpt-6.1-sol} produced candidate computations. The \texttt{codex:gpt-6.1-sol} snapshot was accepted.
 17	\item Proofs: \texttt{claude-sonnet-5-5} (prover).
 18	\item Refereeing: \texttt{claude-opus-5-5} and \texttt{codex:gpt-6.1-sol}. Each proposition was reviewed independently by both.
 19	\item Integration (assembly audit): \texttt{claude-opus-5-5} and \texttt{codex:gpt-6.1-sol}, independently.
 20	\end{itemize}
 21	Claude models ran under Claude Code 2.1.309 and Codex models under codex-cli 0.166.0. This write-up was produced by \texttt{claude-opus-5-5} (Claude Code 2.1.311).
 22	
 23	\emph{Workflow.} The problem statement restricts $f$ to $\Z[t]$. The numerical candidates were computed before any plan existed, and one was accepted on computational grounds only, not because of its fitted constants. Eight proof plans were written, and the plan \texttt{simplest@claude-opus-5-5} was selected by the project owner. It splits the argument into nine propositions P1--P9. In this text, P1 is \cref{prop:densities}, P2 is \cref{prop:identity}, P3 is \cref{prop:pairs}, P4 is \cref{prop:trivial}, P5 is \cref{prop:sawtooth}, P6 is \cref{prop:MT}, P7 is \cref{prop:single}, P8 is \cref{prop:poly}, and P9 is \cref{thm:main} with its proof in \cref{sec:assembly}. All nine proofs passed both referees in the first round, with only cosmetic remarks. Both integrators found that the propositions compose to the stated theorem without loss and without interface mismatches. The project owner accepted the proof as correct and complete. A separate read-only operator audit found no substantive gap and listed local corrections, which are carried out in this write-up (see \texttt{changes.md}) together with the cosmetic points raised by the referees. Agreement between models is evidence, not certification; the arguments are reproduced here in full so that they can be checked directly.
 24	
 25	\emph{Independent checks during the run.} Before acceptance, the operator compared both numerical candidates on overlapping rows and recomputed some rows by brute force. One integrator recomputed all $16$ rows for $k^2+1$ and $k^3+2$ at $x=250000$ by direct counting. The other computed $\sum f'(k)\MT(x;f(k))$ independently and compared it with the data. These checks are recorded in the archive. The computations in \cref{sec:numerics} were redone for this write-up as described there.
 26	
 27	\emph{Literature.} The run used the papers supplied with the problem and literature up to 2010, with the single exception of Brüdern--Wooley~\cite{BruedernWooley2011}, which was supplied. The present text cites only works in the supplied corpus, with one exception: Mirsky~\cite{Mirsky1949}, taken from the project's list of bibliography candidates and not consulted. See \texttt{README.md} for the limitations of the bibliography.
